# No tool attribution

This repository has one golden rule: a commit that names or is authored by a
coding tool must never be created, and must never be pushed.

- Do not add `Co-authored-by` trailers.
- Do not set author or committer to a tool identity.
- Do not write tool names into the commit message. The machine `commit-msg`
  hook already rejects many of those words, including ordinary English that
  happens to match them. Prefer a short human message.
- Never pass `--no-verify`.
- Before `git push`, run `git log --format='%an %ae %cn %ce%n%B' origin/main..HEAD`
  (or the equivalent for a new branch) and confirm every commit is
  `Nomadcxx <noovie@gmail.com>` with a clean message.
- If a tool injected attribution, rewrite locally before any push. A tainted
  commit that has been pushed is a broken release, not a documentation issue.
